Writing an error enum is right for libraries, whose callers may want to react to each cause. In an application's `main` or in quick tools, you often only want to report whatever went wrong. Then use `Box<dyn std::error::Error>`: "a pointer to any kind of error". `?` converts any error type into it.

```rust
use std::error::Error;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let text = std::fs::read_to_string("numbers.txt")?; // io::Error
    let n: i64 = text.trim().parse()?;                  // ParseIntError
    println!("{}", n * 2);
    Ok(())
}
```

The `anyhow` (applications) and `thiserror` (libraries) crates are widely used to remove the boilerplate of both approaches; they build on exactly the traits shown here.
